            <div xml:id="d575">

                <pb facs="0681" n="1515" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1515"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656d.119/56a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-07-18T18:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 18, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>289. For Sheldon from War
                    Trade Board:</p>
                <p>No. 881. Taylor. We have
                    now resumed granting export licenses for the Dutch East Indies, with the
                    exception of iron, steel, and their products, machinery, tin plate, caustic
                    soda, industrial explosives. These reserved items represent in value more than
                    one-half of our total trade. Steamers continue to sail from the Dutch East
                    Indies and are carrying normal quantities of tin and cinchona bark. Under these
                    conditions, we feel inclined to grant export licenses for the above-reserved
                    articles and it is suggested to you that we <pb facs="0682" n="1516"
                        xml:id="pg1516"/> should do so, unless you think it imperative that we
                    should withhold such licenses with the object of facilitating your negotiations
                    in London for the general agreement. It would seem to us, on the other hand,
                    that this is not likely to be a deciding factor and that while it might exert
                    pressure, yet it would not be sufficient to bring a conclusion to the
                    negotiations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
